WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.240
How can we simulate the motion of fluids and smoke?

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:05.460
If we had a block of plastic

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:07.589
in our computer program and we would add

00:00:07.589 --> 00:00:09.269
the laws of physics that control the

00:00:09.269 --> 00:00:11.309
motion of fluids it would immediately

00:00:11.309 --> 00:00:14.390
start behaving like water.

00:00:14.390 --> 00:00:16.340
In these simulations we're mostly

00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:18.320
interested in the velocity and the

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:20.510
pressure of the fluid. How these quantities

00:00:20.510 --> 00:00:22.910
exactly change in time. This we need

00:00:22.910 --> 00:00:25.279
to compute in every point in space, which

00:00:25.279 --> 00:00:26.930
would take an infinite amount of

00:00:26.930 --> 00:00:29.540
resources. What we usually do is we try

00:00:29.540 --> 00:00:31.849
to compute them, not everywhere, but in

00:00:31.849 --> 00:00:33.710
many different places and we try to

00:00:33.710 --> 00:00:35.450
guess these quantities between these

00:00:35.450 --> 00:00:37.790
points. By this guessing a lot of

00:00:37.790 --> 00:00:40.910
information is lost and it still takes a

00:00:40.910 --> 00:00:43.730
lot of resources for a really detailed

00:00:43.730 --> 00:00:46.430
simulation. It is not uncommon that one

00:00:46.430 --> 00:00:48.620
has to wait for days to get only a few

00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:51.199
seconds of video footage. And this is

00:00:51.199 --> 00:00:53.930
where Wavelet Turbulence comes into play.

00:00:53.930 --> 00:00:56.180
We know exactly what frequencies are

00:00:56.180 --> 00:00:58.519
lost and where they are lost and this

00:00:58.519 --> 00:01:00.500
technique enables us to synthesize this

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:02.539
information and add it back very

00:01:02.539 --> 00:01:05.210
cheaply. This way one can get really

00:01:05.210 --> 00:01:07.010
detailed simulations at a very

00:01:07.010 --> 00:01:08.330
reasonable cost.

00:01:08.330 --> 00:01:10.370
Here are some examples of smoke

00:01:10.370 --> 00:01:12.770
simulations with, and without Wavelet

00:01:12.770 --> 00:01:15.920
Turbulence. It really makes a great

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:18.290
difference. It is no accident that the

00:01:18.290 --> 00:01:21.540
technique was a technical Oscar award.

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:23.520
Among many other systems it is

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:25.620
implemented in Blender so anyone can

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:26.580
give it a try.

00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:30.100
Make sure to do so because it's lots of fun.

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:32.240
The paper and the supplementary video is

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:34.400
also available in the description box.

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:37.200
This is an amazing paper, easily one of

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:39.450
my favorites so if you know some math

00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:41.820
make sure to take a look, and if you

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:44.040
don't, just enjoy the footage.

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:51.650
Thank you for watching and see you next time!

